::GEO_U06_UA_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_UA_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which coordinate evidence supports parallel sides? {
=A. Equal slopes#Equal slopes support parallel lines.
~B. Equal areas#Area does not prove side direction.
~C. Equal y-intercepts#Intercepts alone do not prove sides parallel.
~D. Similar colors#Color is not coordinate evidence.
}

::GEO_U06_UA_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_UA_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.9<br><br>Question:<br>Which coordinate tool finds distance between two points? {
~A. Slope formula#Slope gives direction, not distance.
=B. Distance formula#The distance formula finds length between coordinate points.
~C. Area formula#Area formulas measure regions.
~D. Volume formula#Volume formulas measure solids.
}

::GEO_U06_UA_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_UA_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.9<br><br>Question:<br>What is the equation of a circle with center (0,0) and radius 5? {
~A. x + y = 5#This is linear, not a circle equation.
~B. x&sup2; + y&sup2; = 5#The right side should be r&sup2;.
=C. x&sup2; + y&sup2; = 25#A circle centered at the origin has x&sup2;+y&sup2;=r&sup2;.
~D. (x - 5)&sup2; + y&sup2; = 25#This shifts the center to (5,0).
}

::GEO_U06_UA_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_UA_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.10<br><br>Question:<br>What is the center of (x + 3)&sup2; + (y - 4)&sup2; = 16? {
~A. (3,-4)#The signs are reversed.
~B. (3,4)#The x-coordinate sign is wrong.
~C. (-3,-4)#The y-coordinate sign is wrong.
=D. (-3,4)#The equation form gives center (-3,4).
}

::GEO_U06_UA_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_UA_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.10<br><br>Question:<br>A coordinate rectangle is 7 by 2. What is its area? {
=A. 14 square units#Rectangle area is 7(2)=14.
~B. 18 units#That is perimeter, not area.
~C. 9 square units#This adds side lengths.
~D. 28 square units#This doubles the area.
}

::GEO_U06_UA_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_UA_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.13<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has base 8 m and height 5 m. What is its area? {
~A. 40 m&sup2;#This omits the one-half factor.
=B. 20 m&sup2;#Triangle area is one-half base times height.
~C. 13 m&sup2;#This adds the dimensions.
~D. 80 m&sup2;#This doubles the product without reason.
}

::GEO_U06_UA_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_UA_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.15<br><br>Question:<br>Which unit fits surface area? {
~A. cm#Centimeters are linear units.
~B. cm&sup3;#Cubic centimeters measure volume.
=C. cm&sup2;#Surface area uses square units.
~D. degrees#Degrees measure angles.
}

::GEO_U06_UA_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_UA_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.14<br><br>Question:<br>Which unit fits volume? {
~A. cm#Centimeters are linear units.
~B. cm&sup2;#Square centimeters measure area.
~C. degrees#Degrees measure angles.
=D. cm&sup3;#Volume uses cubic units.
}

::GEO_U06_UA_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_UA_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.4<br><br>Question:<br>If scale factor is 2, how does surface area change? Choose the most precise answer. {
=A. 4 times#Surface area changes by k&sup2;, so 2&sup2;=4.
~B. 2 times#That is the length effect.
~C. 8 times#That is the volume effect.
~D. 6 times#This does not use the square of the scale factor.
}

::GEO_U06_UA_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_UA_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.11<br><br>Question:<br>A cylinder is sliced parallel to its base. What is the cross-section? Choose the most precise answer. {
~A. Rectangle#That may occur with a perpendicular slice.
=B. Circle#A base-parallel slice of a cylinder is circular.
~C. Triangle#This slice does not form a triangle.
~D. Parabola#A parabola is a conic section, not this cylinder slice.
}

::GEO_U06_UA_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_UA_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.12<br><br>Question:<br>Which rotation creates a cylinder? Choose the most precise answer. {
~A. Rotating a triangle around a leg#That creates a cone.
~B. Rotating a semicircle around its diameter#That creates a sphere.
=C. Rotating a rectangle around one side#A rectangle rotated around one side creates a cylinder.
~D. Rotating a line around itself#That does not create a cylinder with volume.
}

::GEO_U06_UA_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_UA_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.16<br><br>Question:<br>Which figure is formed by slicing a cone in some ways? Choose the most precise answer. {
~A. Cube#A cube is not a conic section.
~B. Prism#A prism is not a conic section.
~C. Pyramid#A pyramid is not a conic section.
=D. Parabola#A parabola is one possible conic section.
}

